I believe the arrangements were made on Friday, as a matter of fact, to ask for interim supply, but I did not move on that occasion, because the Senate will not be in session until to-morrow and it was not necessary to move at that time.
Motion agreed to and the house went into committee of supply, Mr. Morand in the chair.
1.	Resolved, that a sum not exceeding $16,058,144.05, being one-twelfth of the amount of each of the several items to be voted as set forth in the main estimates for the fiscal year ending 31st March, 1936, laid before the House of Commons at the present session of parliament, be granted to His Majesty on account, for the fiscal year ending 31st March, 1936.
2.	Resolved, that a sum not exceeding $1,063,339.90, being one-twelfth of the amount of each of the several following items to be voted, as set forth in the main estimates for the fiscal year ending 31st March, 1936, laid before the House of Commons at the present session of parliament, be granted to His Majesty, on account, for the fiscal year ending 31st March, 1936, namely:

Votes Nos. 4, 5 and 17, civil government;
Vote No. 82, Hudson Bay railway;
Vote No. 89, canals, improvements;
Vote No. 106, public works, ordnance stores at Calgary;
Votes Nos. 129, 135 and 136, ocean and river service;
Vote No. 139, public works, St. Lawrence dredging;
Votes Nos. 143, 147 and 148, lighthouse and coast service;
Vote No. 158, steamboat inspection;
Vote No. 185, Royal Canadian Mounted Police;
Vote No. 202, public health engineering;
Votes Nos. 235 and 246, miscellaneous; chief electoral offices, battlefields memorial.
3.	Resolved,	that	a	sum	not exceeding
$82,633.33, being one-third of the amount of each of the several following items to be voted, as set forth in the main estimates for the fiscal year ending 31st March, 1936, laid before the House of Commons at the present session of parliament,	be granted to His
Majesty, on account, for the fecal year ending 31st March, 1936, namely: -
Votes Nos.	102	and 107,	public works,
armouries Westmount and Montreal, Vancouver public building;
Vote No. 264, miscellaneous-tourist business.
4.	Resolved,	that	a	sum	not exceeding
$278,083.33, being one-twelfth of the amount of each of the several items to be voted as set forth in the estimates for the fiscal year ending 31st March, 1936, for Canadian National Steamships and Maritime Freight Rates Act, laid before the House of Commons at the present session of parliament, be granted to His Majesty, on account, for the fiscal year ending 31st March, 1936.
Resolutions reported, read the second time, and concurred in.
3226	COMMONS
His Majesty The King-Reply to Address
